<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1))) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i-1)) == null));</code>

However, the above assertion seems a bit off in terms of the logic described in the comment. The correct interpretation should be that for every valid index `i` from the insertion point to the previous size of the vector, the element at position `i` in the updated vector is either null and the corresponding old element at position `i-1` is null, or it is equal to the old element at position `i-1`. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i <= \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1))) || (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i-1)) == null));</code>

But since the shift happens and the element at `index` is set to `obj`, the correct assertion should be:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) || (this.get(i+1) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null));</code>

This correctly reflects that each element from the insertion point onwards is shifted up by one position.
